"""The one place `BazelTrait.exec_log_event` hooks are wired up and fired.

Every task that drives Bazel needs the same three steps, and getting any of them
wrong fails quietly, so none of them live at the call sites:

  `open(trait)`      before the spawn. Returns the `execution_log=` list to pass
                     to `ctx.bazel.build` / `.test` — the trait's own sinks, plus
                     one `bazel.execution_log.iterator()` handle when a hook is
                     registered. The handle has to exist *before* the spawn: it
                     is what tells the runtime an every-entry consumer is
                     subscribed, which is what makes the stream lossless.
  `pump(ctx, h)`     optional, inside the task's existing drain loop. Fires
                     hooks on whatever has arrived, without blocking.
  `close(ctx, h)`    after the task's own drain loop and **before** `build.wait()`.
                     Drains to the end of the stream and fires the rest.

## Progressive dispatch needs a drain loop that runs

`pump` is where a hook hears about an entry *during* the build, and it only
fires as often as the task's loop calls it. The tasks with a `sleep_iter` tick
loop (`lint`, `format`, `gazelle`, `warming`, `delivery`, `run`) call it on every
tick regardless of what Bazel is doing. `bazel/invocation.axl` — `build` and
`test` — instead pumps from `on_event`, so its loop runs only when a BES event
iterator exists, and an `exec_log_event` hook does not by itself create one
(deliberately: a BEP FIFO and a parsed event stream are a real cost to impose on
a feature that does not read build events).

With no event iterator, every hook fires in `close` instead, still before
`wait()` and still with every entry. So the guarantee is completeness, not
timeliness: a hook that only aggregates sees no difference, a hook that reports
progress may report all of it at the end of the build.

Why `close` must precede `wait()`: a bound handle is a subscriber the producer
blocks for, and `wait()` joins the producer. Calling it the other way round would
join a thread that is waiting for us. `build.wait()` releases any handle still
bound for exactly this reason — a task that skips `close` loses entries instead
of hanging — but losing entries is not the contract, so call `close`.

## One task, several invocations

`open` is called once per Bazel invocation, so a task that drives Bazel more
than once dispatches to the same hook more than once — `aspect delivery` wires
phases 1 and 3 (its phase-2 checksum re-run deliberately carries no handle; the
reason is recorded in `crates/aspect-cli/src/builtins/exec_log_wiring.rs`), and
a retry loop opens a fresh handle per attempt. An entry carries nothing
identifying which of those produced it.

`ctx.task.current_phase().name` is what does. A task opens its phase before it
spawns Bazel, which puts the whole window these three verbs cover — `open`,
every `pump`, and `close` — inside that phase: a hook reads `build` under
delivery's phase 1 and `deliver` under its phase 3, `lint` under `lint`,
`populate` under `ci warming`, and `build_retry_<n>` / `test_retry_<n>` on an
attempt after the first. `ExecLogHook` in `./trait.axl` documents it from the
hook's side; `crates/aspect-cli/tests/exec_log_event.rs` asserts the name a hook
actually read at every call site, including that it is never unset.

The one case a phase name cannot separate is two invocations under a single
phase. No task does that today — every retry transitions — and the gap is
additive to close: a per-invocation identifier would arrive as a new accessor,
never as a changed `on_entry` signature.

A second invocation must also not re-write the trait's *file* sinks. Those are
written by Bazel and read by the task's `build_end` hooks, and the artifact
uploader's `build_end` both uploads the execution log and deletes it — a leftover
file leaks its highest-risk artifact, action command lines and environment
variables, with no on-disk redaction. So an invocation that spawns Bazel after
`build_end` has run passes `[.entries]` rather than `.sinks`: the hook dispatch
without the file. Exactly one invocation per run gets `.sinks`, and which one
that is can depend on the run — `aspect delivery` fires `build_end` after phase 2
when phases 1/2 run and after phase 3 when they are skipped, so phase 3 decides
from `phase_1_2_runs` rather than once and for all. A single fixed path could not
hold two invocations' logs anyway; a caller that wants a second invocation's log
on disk has to key the path on the phase.

## Back-pressure

A hook cannot slow Bazel down. In production the CLI does not give Bazel a FIFO:
Bazel writes `--execution_log_compact_file` as a regular file and the CLI tails
it (`ExecLogStream::spawn_with_file`). A slow consumer parks the CLI's reader
thread on a full 1000-entry channel; Bazel keeps writing to the file regardless.
So the cost of an expensive hook is paid at the end of the build, in `close` and
in the `join()` inside `wait()`, and never as a stalled action graph.

The channel is a broadcast: every subscriber receives every entry, so a hook is
an *additional* consumer alongside a `File` or `CompactFile` sink rather than a
competitor for entries. `build.execution_logs()` is the exception — it takes the
stream's own subscriber rather than cloning one, so it errors when anything else
is already reading, a registered hook included.

## `entry.id` does not identify a spawn

Bazel numbers only the entries something else refers to by id — files, input
sets, runfiles trees. A `spawn` is referred to by nothing, so it arrives with
`id = 0`, as every spawn in the log does. Use the resolver's `spawn_key(spawn)`
(its primary output path) to name or deduplicate one;
`@aspect//private/lib/execlog.axl` has it.

What this does not fix is volume. One entry per spawn plus one per file,
directory, input set and runfiles tree is 10k-50k entries on a medium build, and
a Starlark call per entry is real money — hence `ExecLogHook.kinds`, which is
applied in Rust before an entry becomes a Starlark value. `kinds = ["spawn"]` is
commonly a few percent of the stream.
"""

load("@aspect//bazel/trait.axl", "BazelTrait", "ExecLogHook")

# Entries dispatched per `pump` call. Bounds one non-blocking visit so a burst
# cannot monopolize a tick loop that also has BES events and a progress line to
# get to; whatever is left is picked up on the next tick, or by `close`.
_PUMP_BUDGET = 10000

def _wanted_kinds(hook: ExecLogHook) -> list[str]:
    """One hook's kinds, deduplicated and sorted, so two hooks that asked for the
    same set compare equal however each spelled it."""
    return sorted({kind: True for kind in hook.kinds})

def _union_kinds(hooks: list[ExecLogHook]) -> list[str] | None:
    """The kinds the runtime must deliver: the union of what every hook asked
    for, or None for "every kind".

    None is deliberately infectious. A hook with `kinds = []` reads all kinds, so
    narrowing the stream on another hook's behalf would silently starve it — the
    failure mode this whole module exists to avoid. No hooks at all is also None,
    because `kinds = []` is not a value the runtime accepts and an empty union
    must never reach it."""
    out = {}
    for hook in hooks:
        if not hook.kinds:
            return None
        for kind in hook.kinds:
            out[kind] = True
    return sorted(out) if out else None

def _is_exact(hooks: list[ExecLogHook], kinds: list[str] | None) -> bool:
    """Whether the runtime's filter is already exactly right for every hook, so
    dispatch can skip re-checking each entry's kind in AXL.

    True when no hook filters, so every entry is wanted by everyone; or when every
    hook asked for the same set, which is then the set the runtime was given.
    False when hooks disagree, because the union is wider than the narrowest hook
    asked for and delivering it unchecked would hand that hook kinds it never
    asked for — an unfiltered auditor beside a `kinds = ["spawn"]` hook is the
    common shape, and `_union_kinds` reports None for it."""
    if kinds == None:
        return all([not hook.kinds for hook in hooks])
    for hook in hooks:
        if _wanted_kinds(hook) != kinds:
            return False
    return True

def _open(bazel_trait: BazelTrait) -> struct:
    """Resolve this invocation's execution-log wiring. See the module docstring.

    `.sinks` goes straight to `execution_log =`; it is already the trait's
    `execution_log_sinks` plus the hook handle, so a caller passes it instead of
    building that list itself. Falsy when nothing wants the log at all, which is
    what `ctx.bazel.build` needs to skip the stream entirely (an empty list means
    "stream on with no consumer") — hence `xl.sinks or False` at the call sites.

    `.entries` is the handle on its own, `None` when no hook is registered. Only a
    task spawning Bazel a second time after its `build_end` hooks have run passes
    `[.entries]` instead of `.sinks`, to dispatch without re-writing the trait's
    file sinks; see "One task, several invocations" above. It is never both —
    `.sinks` already holds the handle, and binding one twice is an error.

    Call once per Bazel invocation, after the `build_start` hooks: a handle binds
    to a single build, and `build_start` can itself register an `exec_log_event`
    subscription."""
    hooks = list(bazel_trait.exec_log_event)
    sinks = list(bazel_trait.execution_log_sinks)
    if not hooks:
        return struct(sinks = sinks, entries = None, hooks = [], exact = True)

    kinds = _union_kinds(hooks)
    entries = bazel.execution_log.iterator() if kinds == None else bazel.execution_log.iterator(kinds = kinds)
    return struct(
        sinks = sinks + [entries],
        entries = entries,
        hooks = hooks,
        exact = _is_exact(hooks, kinds),
    )

def _wants(hook: ExecLogHook, kind: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `hook` asked for entries of `kind`. `kinds = []` means all."""
    return not hook.kinds or kind in hook.kinds

def _fire(ctx: TaskContext, h: struct, entry) -> None:
    """Hand one entry to every hook that asked for its kind."""
    if h.exact:
        for hook in h.hooks:
            hook.on_entry(ctx, entry)
        return

    # Hooks disagree about kinds, so the runtime's union over-delivers for some
    # of them. One `type()` per entry, not one per hook.
    kind = type(entry.type)
    for hook in h.hooks:
        if _wants(hook, kind):
            hook.on_entry(ctx, entry)

def _pump(ctx: TaskContext, h: struct) -> int:
    """Dispatch what has already arrived and return how many entries that was.
    Never blocks. Safe to call any number of times, including when no hook is
    registered."""
    if h.entries == None:
        return 0
    seen = 0
    for _ in range(_PUMP_BUDGET):
        entry = h.entries.try_pop()
        if entry == None:
            break
        _fire(ctx, h, entry)
        seen += 1
    return seen

def _close(ctx: TaskContext, h: struct) -> None:
    """Drain to the end of the stream, dispatching as entries arrive. Blocks
    until Bazel closes the log, so this belongs after the task's own drain loop
    and before `build.wait()` — see the module docstring for why that order is
    not interchangeable."""
    if h.entries == None:
        return
    for entry in h.entries:
        _fire(ctx, h, entry)

exec_log = struct(
    open = _open,
    pump = _pump,
    close = _close,
    Hook = ExecLogHook,
    testonly_union_kinds = _union_kinds,
    testonly_is_exact = _is_exact,
    testonly_fire = _fire,
    testonly_wants = _wants,
    testonly_wanted_kinds = _wanted_kinds,
)
